{"name":"Shinkansen White","hex":"#dacdcd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shinkansen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Japanese high-speed railway line.","A high-speed passenger train which runs on such a line; a bullet train."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shinkansen"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shinkansen","blocks":[{"text":"The Shinkansen , colloquially known in English as the bullet train, is a network of primarily high-speed railway lines in Japan. The system was developed to provide connections between Tokyo and other regions of the country. In addition to long-distance services, some sections in and around the largest metropolitan areas are used for commuter travel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Shinkansen_map_202405_en.png/330px-Shinkansen_map_202405_en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinkansen"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/